EU health ministers have been studying  plans to revise laws on medical devices  the talks in Luxembourg the ministers  were updated on draft rules for things  like corrective classes dental filling  material x-ray machines heart valves or  breast implants and on in vitro  diagnostic devices such as blood tests  the aim is to define a framework for  production and market circulation of  medical devices restoring trust among  consumers users and stakeholders while  ensuring a high level of protection for  health the council also looked at  nutrition and physical activity given  that up to seven percent of EU health  budgets are directly spent each year on  disease linked to obesity The ministers  express concern at the scale of the  obesity problem they invited you  countries in the European Commission to  better inform citizens about the  benefits of physical activity and a  healthy diet in other items on the  agenda ministers responsible for  employment and Social Policy looked at  the representation of women on company  boards the European Commission wants to  increase the number of women on non  executive boards to forty percent by  2020 EU nations support the principle of  improving the gender balance although  some nations believe this should be done  at the national level while others  prefer eu-wide legislation
 

